Hi, I've spent the last few days playing WazHack. :)

The first few games had me killed really quickly until I remembered
rule #1 (don't run while wounded), after which I reliably got pretty
far. Actually, I've reached Waz twice. While I don't like paying money
for stuff I could get for free, the game is fun enough (good graphics
make so much difference!) I wouldn't mind shelling out 2$. However, I
refuse to use PayPal, so I guess we're out of luck.

First off, I love the graphics (especially the cute animations) and
music/sounds (though the "wounded" sound gets a bit annoying when you
take a lot of damage in a short amount of time). Interface-wise, the
tutorial is extremely well-done and I love the red haze when you're
close to death. I also like how the Z-button allows for longer waiting
until canceled, though I haven't yet worked out what decides whether a
click will wait a short time or trigger a longer wait.

The game is clearly a roguelike (I guess that means that "grid-like"
can be crossed off the list of requirements) and just as clearly
derived from NetHack. All the items (except spellbooks and gems) I've
encountered also existed in NetHack and I quickly reminded that use-
identification is a bad idea in NetHack. I've encountered two wands of
wishing so far, but as I had no idea what items exist in WazHack I
went for the practical stuff and wished for e.g. scrolls of identify,
bread or potions of extra healing.

> =A0- You cannot die by eating too much food
> =A0 =A0 (you will probably still die though, choking is pretty bad)

That's one of my favourite changes. Luckily my sorceress had plenty of
healing (that was the one with the wand of healing), so she actually
survived that one. The biggest problem was that the "ouch" sound is
much too jarring for periodic damage.

> =A0- Wand zapping and bouncing is way more fun with 360 degree freedom

It really is. Playing spellcasters and hunters is awesome! :)

> =A0- Blindness is more 'poor vision' - the screen does not just go blank

That's a really great effect, very creepy and realistic. Definitely
playable (didn't feel crippling in combat or exploration) but annoying
enough I want to cure it anyway.

> =A0- Lamps and keys activate as needed, saving oil and sanity

Not quite true. Lamps need to be readied to work (incidentally, is
there any point to not having that happen automatically?) and keys
bring up a prompt on every single closed door you encounter, which is
more annoying than having to apply the key in the rare case I actually
need to lock a door.

BUGS:

* Sometimes, I can see monsters on the other side of a door. That's
the case for at least floating eyes and giant spores.
* When shooting into a dark corridor, the game nonetheless identifies
the monster I hit.
* A few times I got messages that didn't make sense. Once, I had a
knight fall asleep on his way down into the dungeon (right on the
first ladder), and another time I got "The dwarf looks charismatic"
when there wasn't any dwarf (or any other kind of monster) nearby and
I didn't get to see a dwarf until a few floors further down.
* The wand of magic missile (or striking?) is inscribed as "Nothing
happened", although it clearly shot a beam at a monster and killed it.
* The freshness descriptions of meat chunks seem to be inversed. Fresh
off the corpse, they usually don't get any descriptor at all, some
time later they become "fresh" and afterwards "very fresh".
* Once, I was attacked (and killed) by a shopkeeper, but I've no idea
what I did wrong. I was invisible at the time but I had previously
been able to both sell and buy stuff at his store. I know that
stealing is supposed to be possible, but I don't know how to do that,
so I don't think that's the problem. As far as I remember, there
wasn't even any explanation in the log.
* Sometimes, when I'm standing on a ladder and trying to autotravel
into an adjacent corridor, the character is stuck on the ladder and
keeps walking on the spot until I interrupt it. This only seems to
happen when I'm trying to pick up an item lying close to but not
immediately next to a ladder.
* If I pick up an item in a shop but decide to give it up, the button
is labeled "Sell", which doesn't make sense as the item in question
already belongs to the shopkeeper. (I do realize this actually is the
selling menu, but in this case it seems weird.)

FEATURE REQUESTS:

* Please, would you consider some kind of auto-pickup feature for
arrows? Ranged fighting (also wands and spells) is ever so much fun,
but having to click on all the arrows is getting tedious.
* Similarly, throwing stuff is extremely annoying (click on inventory,
scroll down to your stack of daggers, click on the daggers and select
"Throw" in the context menu). At the very least, could the equipment
menu please include a context menu to "ready" a stack of items in
addition to wielding them?
* Auto-switching back to my main weapon after throwing would be nice,
but could be tricky if there are monsters around.
* Minor annoyance: If I'm trying to wield a stack of items, I get
failure messages for each of them I fail to wield (i.e. N-2, seeing
how I only have two hands). Limiting that to just a single message
would be nice.
* As mentioned above, please get rid of the automatic "lock this door"
prompt once you carry a key. It's much more likely that I simply want
to open the door instead. If you want to go fancy, you could include a
check whether I'd recently closed the door.
* This one's a bit more extensive and pretty minor, I guess, but it
would be cool if they mini-map could include a glyph for each of the
special rooms. That's the only thing that's worth keeping track of.

GENERAL QUESTIONS and COMMENTS:

* Why do cats and dogs refuse to eat the corpses? Also, do they ever
grow into their adult animal form?
* So far I haven't figured out the purpose of being able to jump,
though it _is_ fun to see your character hop along corridors. :)
* It took me a while to work out how to use portal stones, but they're
a really cool addition.
* I love the randomized character names that are not quite "real" and
not quite "fantastic" names, also the titles matching the classes.
* Chatting with walls is a bit unintuitive.


Great game, thanks!

